\section{Introduction and state of the art}

In "Zen and the Art of Genetic Algorithms", \cite{Zen}, Goldberg lays out a series of {\em koans} or principles for nature-inspired metaheuristics: behind "Let nature be your guide", which goes first, and "Trust implicit parallelism" goes "The Middle Way": "Balance exploration and exploitation". This balance of exploration and exploitation has been, since then, one of the founding principles of metaheuristic design, implying that you need to have operators that combine individuals with high fitness, as well as others that "break" them moving the population to new, random areas of the search space; this also follows the 4th principle, "Do not fear destruction".

This has been widely understood as the need to balance the application rate of these two kinds of operators. However, {\em balancing} is not a simple thing; in practice, there's a single point where a system reaches  balance; {\em a priori}, one would think that striking balance is well-nigh impossible. Furthermore, there is not a consensus on {\em where} exploration and exploitation actually occur \cite{eiben98}, other than admitting that mutation is almost purely an exploration operator.
% También ahí puede haber un balance. Por ejemplo, en casos continuos una mutación gaussiana con dispersión chica, o un solo bit-flip en el caso de binarios, no aumentan mucho la diversidad y es más bien para buscar localmente. Pero si cambias esos parámetros tendrán mayor efecto en la exploración. Por eso, en algunos papers los movíamos a lo largo de la búsqueda o cambiábamos en las distintas poblaciones. Crossover igual, puede agregar mucha o poca diversidad, por ejemplo, con el tamaño del torneo. - M
In many cases, the nature of an operator is related to its action on {\em schemata}, or blocks that perform better than average; {\em exploitative} operators keep these schemata, while {\em explorative} disrupt or create new ones. There are some issues with this definition, including the fact that in most cases the algorithm will be working on real-valued chromosomes where the concept of schemata is not so helpful.

This lack of formal definition of the two terms, or precision about where they occur, certainly does not help \cite{Crepinsek2013exploration} gather insights into the inner workings of algorithms or exactly where the core of their success lies. In general, a rule of thumb indicates that if evolution is stagnated, there is too much exploration of parts of the search space that are far away from the solutions; on the other hand, if diversity is high, exploration 
% do you mean exploration? exploitation will mean less diversity. - Mario
% No, exploitation; I've explained it a bit.
% But then, diversity must be slow. You say: if diversity is high then exploitation is working ok, but when diversity is high, exploration is increased. - M
% I think we are talking about the same, so I changed the word exploitation for exploration. - M
is working correctly, because it is generating individuals that sample different parts of the search space without falling into a local minimum; this is why, in general, experiments that try to examine this balance tend to work with diversity, measured as fitness or chromosome space entropy. \cite{Crepinsek2013exploration} surveys different ways of controlling and maintaining diversity using different techniques such as learning in order to keep this balance.

There are two issues with these rules of thumb. While it is true that lack of diversity is a symptom of stagnation, high diversity might be caused by excessive exploration, that is, generation of new individuals in unpromising areas of the search space, as well as good exploitation, generation of a sufficient amount of high-fitness individuals in promising areas. In practice, there is no way of distinguishing {\em where} these new, different individuals are created and if the area of the search space they occupy is promising or not through a simple measure such as diversity.

This leaves researchers with a methodological gap: overall, when new metaheuristic algorithms are designed and new operators are introduced, they can only follow certain rules of thumb, like measuring diversity \cite{HASHIM2019646,merelo2022brave-anon} to ensure that there is a correct balance between exploration and exploitation, and even with high diversity, fitness might not reach an optimum value.

This is why in this paper we propose a new methodology for investigating the exploration-exploitation balance: using the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell (FWL) \cite{Lovell01121963} theorem to model the relationship between algorithm parameters and fitness, mediated by the number of evaluations. We can agree that better algorithms will lead to better fitness with fewer evaluations. At the end of the day, what we pursue with algorithm design is precisely that: obtaining efficient algorithms from the computational (and also energetic) point of view. A statistical model of fitness mediated by the number of evaluations and generations
% The evaluations-mediated model? remove the (), number of generations is included, but is only a factor. - Mario
% OK, clarified a bit
will help us find out how much fitness improvement comes from more exploration (more evaluations) or better algorithm adjustment through different parameters. Although this is not exactly a clear exploration/exploitation division, in practice, through parameter adjustment and how they act on the population (exploring, exploiting, or both at the same time), we can try to understand better how these mechanisms work, and adjust the algorithms so that they leverage these insights.

We will apply this model to different design and parameter choices in the Brave New Algorithm \cite{merelo2022brave-anon}, a stratified-population memetic algorithm that was designed with the EEB in mind. For this reason, we need to assess the overall and parametric design in order to ascertain how the number of evaluations influences the achieved result in terms of fitness. We will use the model to gather insights on the exploration and exploitation mechanisms and how they interact or counteract each other.

The rest of the paper is organized as follows: next we present
background and previous work in Section \ref{sec:soa}; then, we proceed to
describe the experimental design (Section \ref{sec:bna}), followed by the experimental results
(Section \ref{sec:exp});
finally, we discuss these results and draw our conclusions in Section \ref{sec:conclusions}.

\section{State of the art}\label{sec:soa}

From the initial paper by Eiben and Schippers \cite{eiben98}, and despite having been a tenet of evolutionary computation from the beginning, there is not an extensive literature that looks at the so-called exploration/exploitation balance. In general, they are seen as opposing forces that need to be kept in balance. \cite{Crepinsek2013exploration} surveys how they are done at a low operator level, how they are controlled, and how the balance is actually achieved. It is not the final word on the subject; for instance, it states that more research is needed to understand how different operators contribute to exploration/exploitation, and it calls for more direct measures. In general, it opens several paths to better understanding its interaction with representation, how different phases of search affect it, and eventually how effective any measure of that balance can be applied to improvement of search. In general, it leaves more questions open than it closes, even as it establishes diversity as the best way to represent EEB and, eventually, to control it.

Recent approaches have tackled the problem differently, however. An article by Ferhat et al. \cite{ferhat2025measures} focuses mainly on measures of diversity and convergence rates; however, these are at most indirect measures that do not really reflect how exploration and exploitation affect fitness improvement. A novel measure of exploration and exploration is proposed in \cite{math14091406} where exploitation is intra-peak motion in search space, and exploitation between peak. % This is not very clear. ( and exploratiton searches the space between peaks?)  - M 
This is also applied to other algorithms \cite{math14173119}, but even if this is an operative way of understanding these measures, it needs to assume and model search space in order to apply them; in this search space models such as the one proposed in \cite{laredo20} can help, providing more efficient sampling methods for what they call {\em exploitation}.

From a slightly different point of view, Rojas et al. \cite{ANOVA} look at the influence of different parameters and operator types on the resulting fitness; they are also interested on diversity and how parameters affect them. They analyze different factors: population size, crossover and mutation probability, as well as different types of selection, crossover and mutation operators. They conclude that these operators, as well as mutation rate, are the ones with the biggest influence on diversity, implying that they are the ones that affect the EEB.

In this paper, however, we will try to attack the problem of optimizing the improvement of fitness per individual evaluation, without the reference to specific search space models or even to a balance between two opposing forces.

\section{A brave new algorithm} \label{sec:bna}

Far from trying to obscure the meaning of a metaheuristic by using a metaphor \cite{sorensen}, our description of a Brave New Algorithm (BNA), based in the novel Brave New World, by Aldous Huxley \cite{huxley2022brave}, searches to simplify the explanation of a stratified evolutionary algorithm (EA) by giving every {\em stratum} a name that is relatively easy to understand for those familiar with this book.
Thus, we are not so much proposing a new metaheuristic as a new way of structuring the population and the reproduction process, which can be applied to any metaheuristic that uses a population of individuals, in such a way that recombination of individuals and random generation is performed in a different way by each stratum.

Our stated objective is to try and find an easier way to disentangle exploration from exploration by keeping some layers explicitly exploratory and some of them either more balanced or simply exploiting; in practice, this means that mutation and crossover will be distributed in a different way depending on the layer or {\em stratum}.
The algorithm is described in more detail in \cite{merelo2022brave-anon,bna25-anon}, but we will give a brief description here for the sake of completeness.

What BNA introduces with respect to other EAs is the division of the population into {\em castes} (i.e., strata);
each receiving a differentiated treatment for reproduction.
More precisely, reproduction goes as follows by caste:
\begin{itemize}
\item The $\alpha$ caste will mate only with other individuals of the same caste, undergoing (possibly) mutation and then crossover. Since this stratum is mainly recombining high-fitness individuals, it is mainly a {\em exploitative} one {\em a priori}.
\item The $\beta$ caste will reproduce only with members of the $\alpha$ caste, again with mutation and crossover. In this case, the exploitative element is more important than the explorative.
\item The $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ castes will only reproduce via cloning and mutation, so they are purely explorative.
\item The $\gamma$ caste will use hill-climbing for a number of steps to improve its fitness. In a EEB framework, it is difficult to qualify this stratum; although it is sampling the space randomly, it will stop when fitness is no longer improving. The issues with pigeonholing some operators this way is one of the reasons why we are looking at the fitness/evaluations relationship from a different angle.
\end{itemize}

Mutation and crossover rates can be established independently for every caste, although by default all mutation rates will be the same.

All individuals will be ranked according to their fitness and then assigned to the castes;
in particular, the $\alpha$ caste will receive the best while the $\gamma$ caste will get only the last ones; percentages assigned to every caste will be an algorithm parameter, although in order to simplify we will only change the $\alpha$ percentage, with $\delta$ and $\epsilon$ getting always 5\%; $\beta$ is always double that of $\alpha$ and finally $\gamma$ the rest; this gives us a single parameter to regulate this aspect of the algorithm, which is strongly related to the exploration/exploitation balance. Selection, usually considered a {\em exploitative} operator, would still be acting here; however, it also selects which operators are going to be applied by placing individuals in different strata, some of them purely explorative. Again, it is difficult to place it in a EEB framework.

The algorithm uses elitism to keep the best member of the previous population
(replacing the worst one) but other than that, all individuals have offspring one way or the other. This is the main difference with respect to other EAs: the worst elements are not dropped from the reproduction pool but mutated in a random or directed way (in the case of $\gamma$). This is intended to keep a diverse population, striking a good exploration/exploitation balance again. This diversity can be enhanced by using differential mutation rates for the different castes;
while
this adds another parameter for experimental design,
it allows customization for specific problems or problem sizes.

The free parameters are thus:
\begin{itemize}
\item Population size:
Note that all castes need to have a non-zero integer size, which limits the possible values for this parameter due to the relationship between the population size for $\alpha$ and $\beta$ caste.
\item $\alpha$ percentage: {\em a priori}
the essential parameter for controlling the balance between exploitation and exploration.
\item Mutation rates
(for all castes except $\gamma$): these can be set to the same value or customized for every caste.
\item Local-search depth: maximum number of hill-climbing {\em steps} while there is still improvement in the fitness.
\item Stopping criterion: the maximum number of generations without improvement, that is, {\em max\_gens}.
\end{itemize}

Note that the orchestrated use of local improvement within this general evolutionary engine makes BNA fall within the general scope of memetic algorithms \cite{cotta2025harnessing}. %
The algorithm is written in Julia \cite{perkel2019julia} and released as open source \cite{Ceci2021bnea-anon}. Julia is a language designed with a JIT compiler to be fast and introduces native concurrent execution. It has been already used in a number of evolutionary algorithm implementations such as \cite{sanchez2023evolp}. In our case, this parallel execution feature matches well the multi-caste evolution of
BNA, opening new venues of research as well as energy-saving. In this paper, however, we are still profiling and optimizing at the single-processor level.

\section{Experiments and results}\label{sec:exp}

We have used the an AMD Ryzen 9 9950X 16-Core Processor running Ubuntu 25.10 with kernel {\tt 6.17.0-19-generic}. The experiments will be run from a Perl script, capturing output from {\sf pinpoint} and saving it to a CSV file. These files are available from GitHub and also from this paper's repository. We make 30 executions for every parameter combination. For every experiment, we save the following data:\begin{itemize}
\item Algorithm parameters: {\tt max\_gens}, that is, maximum number of generations without change before stopping, {\tt steps}, the maximum number of hill-climbing steps in the $\gamma$ caste, {\tt alpha}, which is the percentage of individuals that are going to be placed in the $\alpha$ caste, and {\tt population\_size}.
\item Number of generations that have been actually run
\item Number of evaluations, which include also those made during the hillclimbing step.
\end{itemize}

Please take into account that these experiments have an algorithm-driven, not fixed budget, termination condition. When the population is stagnated, it will no longer improve fitness, which forces the algorithm to stop via the {\em max\_gen} condition.

For this paper, we have focused on a single problem and a single dimension: The Sphere floating-point fitness function included in the BBOB benchmark \cite{hansen2010comparing} for 10 dimensions. The reason we have not introduced more functions and dimensions is that we wanted to focus on the effect of the operating conditions (measured through running time and temperatures) and the algorithm parameters as well as results (fitness, evaluations). Introducing more degrees of freedom would not give us more insights into this model, and it might confuse the interpretation of results. Statistical analysis, however, provides the certainty we need to draw sound conclusions from this set of experiments. Although not a multimodal problem, it is a baseline for understanding how the EEB works and how it is affected by the algorithm versions, its implementations and its parameters.

Our intention is to try, with experiments, to understand the dependence of fitness reached on algorithm parameters as well as implementation and algorithm versions in order to check if it is possible at all to reach lower levels of fitness spending less or the same amount of evaluations. Ultimately, we want to understand energy consumption by algorithm implementations, of which an important component is the number of evaluations and the running time needed for them.

The four parameters and the levels we will be changing are:\begin{itemize}
\item $\alpha$ percentage with two values: 10\% and 25\%. Since it is the main lever for the exploitation/exploration balance, we are looking for better fitness values with less space exploration (less evaluations).
\item The number or {\sf steps} for hill-climbing in the $\gamma$ caste is related to
exploitation. We will test 16 and 64 steps.
\item The number of generations without improvement to stop or {\sf max\_gens} will be set to 10 and 25; more generations allow more exploration and the possibility to go up a notch in fitness, but it can also mean generating more useless solutions.
\item Population size is going to be set to 400 and 800; larger population means more initial diversity giving it a better chance to exploit existing values, but also more exploration.
\end{itemize}

This gives us 8 different parameter settings times 30 for every algorithm/implementation configuration. To ensure we cover different operating conditions, we repeat every set 4 times after at least one reboot; the distance between one set of experiments and the next is random.
We have an open science policy and therefore, all code, experimental results and analyses are available under a free license at our repository \cite{Ceci2021bnea-anon}.

These are the five versions of the algorithm we will test with the same set of parameters: \begin{itemize}
\item The first batch of experiments will be used as baseline; the mutation rate for every caste will be 40\%, meaning that, on average, 4 components of every individual mutated will change randomly within a certain range. This is the default version of the algorithm, named {\sf baseline}
\item The second batch will lower mutation rate to 10\%, meaning that when changing a chromosome, only one element of the chromosome will be changed. While this might seem an obvious way of reducing random search, (i.e., exploration). We are calling this batch {\sf low\_mutation}
\item The {\sf microopt} version in principle changes only the algorithm implementation by performing a series of micro-optimizations at the framework and programming level: eliminate unneeded libraries, and other low-level programming adjustments.
\item The {\sf gamma} variant makes a slight change in the selection process, making the $\gamma$ layer the {\em third} one instead of the last one; by applying hillclimbing to individuals that theoretically have a higher ranking, it should be able to exploit solutions faster.
\item the {\sf no\_alpha\_mutation} version eliminates mutation for the alpha layer, making it a crossover-only stratum. Theoretically, this should reduce exploration and make it a pure exploitation layer
\end{itemize}

<<evoapps27.setup, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.cap="Log-log plot of the residuals of delta fitness vs. number of evaluations for the 4 experiments and baseline", fig.height=3.5 >>=

library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)

N_BOOT <- as.integer(Sys.getenv("N_BOOT", "500"))

# ---- 1. Data ------------------------------------------------------------------

versions <- c(baseline          = "ppsn_speedup_fixed_gradient_descent",
              gamma_upgrade     = "gamma_upgrade",
              microopt          = "ppsn_microopt",
              low_mutation      = "ppsn_low_mutation",
              no_mutation_alpha = "ppsn_no_alpha")

load_version <- function(name, object) {
  env <- new.env()
  load(file.path("data", paste0(object, ".rds")), envir = env)
  get(object, envir = env) %>%
    filter(!startsWith(work, "base")) %>%
    select(population_size, max_gens, alpha, steps, diff_fitness, evaluations) %>%
    mutate(group = name)
}

fitness_data <- bind_rows(Map(load_version, names(versions), versions)) %>%
  mutate(group     = factor(group, levels = names(versions)),
         log_fit   = log10(diff_fitness),
         log_evals = log10(evaluations),
         across(c(alpha, steps, max_gens, population_size), factor))

params <- c("alpha", "steps", "max_gens", "population_size")
param_terms <- paste0("(", paste(params, collapse = " + "), ")^2")

# ---- 2. Pooled models: total effect, mediator, direct effect + slopes ---------

f_total <- as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ group *", param_terms))
f_med   <- as.formula(paste("log_evals ~ group *", param_terms))
f_out   <- as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ group *", param_terms, "+ group:log_evals"))

m_total <- lm(f_total, data = fitness_data)
m_med   <- lm(f_med,   data = fitness_data)
m_out   <- lm(f_out,   data = fitness_data)

# cat("\nR^2  total:", round(summary(m_total)$r.squared, 3),
#     " mediator:", round(summary(m_med)$r.squared, 3),
#     " outcome (parameter + evals):", round(summary(m_out)$r.squared, 3), "\n")

# Convergence rate (log-log slope) per version, with 95% CI
slope_ci <- confint(m_out)[grep(":log_evals$", names(coef(m_out))), , drop = FALSE]
slopes <- data.frame(group = sub("^group(.*):log_evals$", "\\1", rownames(slope_ci)),
                    slope = coef(m_out)[rownames(slope_ci)],
                    lower = slope_ci[, 1], upper = slope_ci[, 2], row.names = NULL)
# cat("\nlog10(diff_fitness) vs log10(evaluations) slope per version (more negative = more efficient):\n")
# print(slopes, digits = 3)


# Partial-regression view of the slopes: strip the parameter effects from both
# axes within each version (Frisch-Waugh-Lovell), so the line through the points
# has exactly the coefficient in `slopes`. Group means are added back to keep
# the axes in real log10 units.
f_y_par <- as.formula(paste("log_fit ~",   param_terms))
f_x_par <- as.formula(paste("log_evals ~", param_terms))

partial <- fitness_data %>%
  group_by(group) %>%
  group_modify(function(d, key) {
    d <- droplevels(d)
    mutate(d,
           fit_adjusted   = resid(lm(f_y_par, d)) + mean(log_fit),
           evals_adjusted = resid(lm(f_x_par, d)) + mean(log_evals))
  }) %>%
  ungroup()

slope_labels <- slopes %>%
  mutate(group = factor(group, levels = names(versions)),
         label = sprintf("slope %.2f [%.2f, %.2f]", slope, lower, upper))

ggplot(partial, aes(evals_adjusted, fit_adjusted, colour = group)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.2, size = 0.7) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x, se = TRUE, linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_text(data = slope_labels, aes(label = label),
            x = -Inf, y = -Inf, hjust = -0.05, vjust = -0.6,
            size = 2.8, colour = "grey20", inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~ group, nrow = 1) +
  labs(x = "log10(evaluations) net of parameters",
       y = "log10(diff_fitness) net of parameters") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "none", panel.grid.minor = element_blank())

@

We have processed the dataset by applying a $log10$ to the number of evaluations and the difference between the fitness reached and optimum fitness. We will model the resulting logarithmid $\Delta$ fitness in terms of the number of evaluations, that is considered the {\em mediator} variable. The Frisch-Waugh-Lovell theorem ) \cite{Lovell01121963} affirms that the coefficients of the dependent variable (in this case $log \Delta$ Fitness) is related to the part of the of the explanatory variable (evaluations) that is not related to the other explanatory variables (algorithm parameters). We then proceed to the decomposition via a linear model of the $log\Delta$ Fitness) and $log$ evaluations, and compute the slope independently for every experiment level.
What we plot in Figure \ref{fig:evoapps27.setup} are the residuals of fitting fitness and evaluations to the rest of the parameters, adjusted to their corresponding scale by adding the average fitness and evaluations value for every group.

This is where we begin to understand the exploration and exploitation balance. However, we have a different angle: the fitness obtained will have two components: the part that can be explained by the parameters in a direct way and the indirect effect, which is the number of {\em extra} evaluations that cannot be directly explained by the parameters. In part, this effect is going to be random, but in fact what is indicating is the extra area of the search space these parameters are placing the population into and that would allow the extra generations until stagnation and the hillclimbing $\gamma$ to effectively produce individuals that continue improving the fitness. We will explore this effect further.

We can clearly see already that the {\sf no\_mutation\_alpha} reaches the lowest fitness values. Although it does so using more evaluations, the slope indicates that, for the same number of evaluations, this configuration is able to optimize fitness more efficiently than the rest of the configurations.

<<evoapps27.fwl, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=5, fig.pos="h!tb", fig.cap="Across configuration (top row) and across runs (bottom row) FWL decomposition of log(evaluations) vs. log(fitness)">>=
# Sanity check: operating conditions should NOT explain fitness. Only meaningful
# if temperatures are added to fitness_data; left here as a reminder.

# ---- 3. Slopes across configurations vs across runs ---------------------------
#
# across_configs: slope on a configuration's mean log10(evaluations), i.e. what
#   a configuration that spends more evaluations gains (no parameter terms in
#   the model, so it also absorbs direct parameter effects).
# across_runs: slope on a run's deviation from its configuration mean, i.e.
#   what a run gains by happening to last longer (seed + when max_gens fires).

fitness_data <- fitness_data %>%
  group_by(group, alpha, steps, max_gens, population_size) %>%
  mutate(log_evals_config_mean = mean(log_evals),
         log_evals_run_dev     = log_evals - log_evals_config_mean) %>%
  ungroup()

m_configs_runs <- lm(log_fit ~ group * (log_evals_config_mean + log_evals_run_dev),
                     data = fitness_data)

config_run_slopes <- data.frame(t(sapply(levels(fitness_data$group), function(g) {
  ref <- g == levels(fitness_data$group)[1]
  b <- coef(m_configs_runs)
  c(across_configs = unname(b["log_evals_config_mean"] + if (ref) 0 else b[paste0("group", g, ":log_evals_config_mean")]),
    across_runs    = unname(b["log_evals_run_dev"]     + if (ref) 0 else b[paste0("group", g, ":log_evals_run_dev")]))
})))
#cat("\nSlopes across configurations (set by parameters, max_gens included) vs",
#    "across runs of the same configuration (seed, realized run length):\n")
#print(config_run_slopes, digits = 3)

# Plot of the two slopes. Top row: one point per configuration (its mean
# log10 evaluations and mean log10 fitness, size = number of runs). Bottom row:
# one point per run, as its distance from its configuration's means. A
# run-weighted line through the top row and a plain line through the bottom
# row reproduce across_configs and across_runs exactly.
config_points <- fitness_data %>%
  group_by(group, alpha, steps, max_gens, population_size) %>%
  summarise(x = mean(log_evals), y = mean(log_fit), runs = n(), .groups = "drop") %>%
  mutate(view = "across configurations")

run_points <- fitness_data %>%
  group_by(group, alpha, steps, max_gens, population_size) %>%
  mutate(x = log_evals_run_dev, y = log_fit - mean(log_fit), runs = 1) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  mutate(view = "across runs\n(distance from mean)")

views <- c("across configurations", "across runs\n(distance from mean)")
config_run_points <- bind_rows(config_points, run_points) %>%
  mutate(view = factor(view, levels = views))

config_run_labels <- config_run_slopes %>%
  mutate(group = factor(rownames(config_run_slopes), levels = names(versions))) %>%
  tidyr::pivot_longer(c(across_configs, across_runs), names_to = "view", values_to = "slope") %>%
  mutate(view  = factor(views[match(view, c("across_configs", "across_runs"))], levels = views),
         label = sprintf("slope %.2f", slope))

ggplot(config_run_points, aes(x, y, colour = group)) +
  geom_point(data = filter(config_run_points, view == views[2]),
             alpha = 0.15, size = 0.6) +
  geom_point(data = filter(config_run_points, view == views[1]),
             aes(size = runs), alpha = 0.8) +
  geom_smooth(aes(weight = runs), method = "lm", formula = y ~ x,
              se = FALSE, linewidth = 0.8, colour = "grey20") +
  facet_wrap(view ~ group, nrow = 2, scales = "free",
             labeller = label_value) +
  scale_size_area(max_size = 3, guide = "none") +
  labs(x = "log10(evaluations)", y = "log10(diff_fitness)") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "none", panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
        axis.text   = element_text(size = 6),
        axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90, vjust = 0.5, hjust = 1),
        strip.text  = element_text(size = 6))
@

The effect of the FWL regression are shown in Figure \ref{fig:evoapps27.fwl}, where we split the effects of the parameters, plotting the averages for every parameter combination, and the indirect effect via more evaluations, both with the slope. This slope measures the direct and indirect contribution to the reached fitness value. Looking at the scales on the top and bottom charts, we can compare the relative effect of direct and indirect effects. For all experiments but the last, the effect on fitness is within the same order of magnitude; in the case of {\sf no\_alpha\_mutation} there are almost two orders of magnitude from the best to the worst effect. The same happens to the indirect effect plotted in the lower row: the one achieved by the last level exceeds what is achieved by the rest. In all cases, however, the same-budget effect (via parameters) is more important than the extra-budget effect; however, they complement each other in achieving optimal results.

This is one reason why these experiments are performed until the termination condition is met: it allows us to understand better how direct and indirect effects combine to achieve the final fitness we observe.

<<evoapps27.param.comparison, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, cache=TRUE,fig.height=6, fig.pos="h!tb", fig.cap="Effects of different parameter values (low or high) on evaluations or fitness for the different experiment levels (left to right) and algorithm parameters (top to bottom)">>=
# Same configuration points, one row per parameter, coloured by its level.
# Arrows join configurations that differ only in that parameter (low -> high):
# along the across_configs slope = it acts by spending more evaluations;
# vertical = it changes fitness at the same cost; ~zero length = no effect.
# Arrows that all point the same way = the better value is always the same.
config_by_param <- bind_rows(lapply(params, function(p) {
  d <- config_points
  d$param      <- p
  d$level      <- as.character(d[[p]])
  d$level_rank <- ifelse(as.integer(d[[p]]) == 1, "low", "high")
  d$others     <- do.call(paste, c(lapply(d[setdiff(params, p)], as.character), sep = "/"))
  d
})) %>%
  mutate(param = factor(param, levels = params))

config_arrows <- config_by_param %>%
  select(group, param, others, level_rank, x, y) %>%
  tidyr::pivot_wider(names_from = level_rank, values_from = c(x, y)) %>%
  filter(!is.na(x_low), !is.na(x_high))

param_level_text <- config_by_param %>%
  group_by(group, param) %>%
  summarise(label = paste0("low = ",  first(level[level_rank == "low"]),
                           ", high = ", first(level[level_rank == "high"])),
            .groups = "drop")

ggplot(config_by_param, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_segment(data = config_arrows,
               aes(x = x_low, y = y_low, xend = x_high, yend = y_high),
               arrow = arrow(length = unit(0.12, "cm")),
               colour = "grey60", linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = level_rank, size = runs), alpha = 0.85) +
  geom_text(data = param_level_text, aes(label = label),
            x = -Inf, y = Inf, hjust = -0.05, vjust = 1.3,
            size = 2.5, colour = "grey30", inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~ param + group, ncol = length(versions), scales = "free",
             labeller = label_value) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c(low = "#1b9e77", high = "#d95f02"), name = "Level") +
  scale_size_area(max_size = 3, guide = "none") +
  labs(x = "configuration mean log10(evaluations)",
       y = "configuration mean log10(diff_fitness)") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
        axis.text   = element_text(size = 6),
        axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90, vjust = 0.5, hjust = 1),
        strip.text  = element_text(size = 6))
@

Figure \ref{fig:evoapps27.param.comparison} compares the effects of the values of different parameters across configurations, showing how the values of the parameters influence directly the quality of the solution: a line from top-right to bottom left will indicate that change in value influences positively the result. This happens for every configuration and the {\sf alpha} value except for the {\sf low\_mutation} version. In all cases, {\sf alpha} = 25\% will obtain better fitness with less evaluations as a {\em direct} result. It should be noted too that the number of steps in the hillclimbing performed in the $\gamma$ layer has a smaller influence, and not clearly in any direction, than the other parameters.

The results for the rest of the parameters are, however, not so clear, with some combinations of population, for instance, having an effect on the number of evaluations (smaller population meaning less evaluations), but not in general. This is why the {\em indirect} effect becomes more important.

<<evoapps27.table, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, cache=TRUE, results="asis">>=
# ---- FINDINGS so far (2026-11-07), to move to the paper -----------------------
# diff_fitness is distance to the optimum: lower = better. Slopes are log-log,
# so 10x more evaluations -> 10^|slope| times better fitness.
#
# F1. Model fit (section 2, pooled). Version + parameters explain 97.9% of the
#     variance of log10(diff_fitness) and 86.4% of log10(evaluations). Adding
#     evaluations raises it to 98.7%: +0.008, i.e. ~38% of what parameters
#     leave unexplained. That extra part is run-to-run variation within a
#     configuration, not a parameter effect. (R^2 values are fit, not a
#     total = direct + indirect decomposition; that is sections 4-5.)
#
# F2. Convergence rate at fixed configuration (FWL slopes, section 2):
#     baseline -0.32, gamma_upgrade -0.38, microopt -0.33, low_mutation -1.24,
#     no_mutation_alpha -2.68. microopt ~ baseline: the implementation-only
#     negative control behaves as expected. The jump comes with low_mutation and
#     grows with no_mutation_alpha: in those versions a run that lasts longer
#     keeps improving much more (10x evaluations -> ~17x and ~480x better fitness,
#     vs ~2x in the first three versions).
#
# F3. Across configurations vs across runs (this section):
#                       across_configs  across_runs
#     baseline              -0.150        -0.324
#     gamma_upgrade         -0.155        -0.377
#     microopt              -0.144        -0.329
#     low_mutation          -0.440        -1.237
#     no_mutation_alpha     -1.010        -2.683
#     In every version, extra evaluations obtained by choosing a different
#     configuration buy 2.2-2.8x less fitness than extra evaluations a run gets
#     by lasting longer. Caveats: across_configs has no parameter terms, so it
#     also absorbs direct parameter effects; across_runs is steepened by the
#     stopping rule (a run that keeps improving is the one that does not stop).
#     No CIs yet; across_configs CIs need a bootstrap over configurations.
#     across_runs matches the F2 slopes, as expected.
#
# F4. Per-parameter view (arrow plot above; visual reading, to be confirmed
#     with mediation_table from section 4):
#     - steps (16 vs 64) has ~no effect: configurations differing only in
#       steps overlap, which produces the point pairs in every panel.
#     - High alpha (25% vs 10%), high max_gens (25 vs 10) and high
#       population_size (800 vs 400) give better fitness in most versions.
#       The arrow plot alone was misleading about alpha; see F5.
#     - no_mutation_alpha breaks the configuration layout shared by the other
#       versions; F5 shows why (alpha's direct effect is ~9x larger there).
#
# F5. Mediation per parameter (section 4; effect of low -> high on
#     log10(diff_fitness), negative = better; direct (same_budget) = at the
#     same number of evaluations, mediated (via_budget) = through the
#     mediator, the evaluation budget).
#     - steps: no effect on either path.
#     - max_gens: pure budget effect. direct ~0, indirect -0.11 to -0.13
#       (first three versions), -0.36 (low_mutation), -0.69
#       (no_mutation_alpha). More patience -> more evaluations -> better
#       fitness, with no better search.
#     - population_size: direct POSITIVE (worse at the same number of
#       evaluations: +0.04 to +0.06, +0.35 low_mutation, +0.30
#       no_mutation_alpha), indirect negative (more evaluations). It pays off
#       only by making runs longer; in low_mutation the two cancel out (total
#       +0.013, n.s.). Reading: an exploration knob, costs efficiency per
#       evaluation.
#     - alpha: direct strongly NEGATIVE (better at the same budget: -0.14 to
#       -0.15, -0.32 low_mutation, -1.33 no_mutation_alpha = ~21x better),
#       indirect POSITIVE (+0.08 to +0.09, +0.36, +0.55): a larger alpha caste
#       means FEWER evaluations, because the run stagnates and the stopping
#       rule fires earlier. Net gain except in low_mutation (total +0.046, worse):
#       there early stagnation outweighs the better search. Reading: an
#       exploitation knob; classic premature-convergence trade-off. With low
#       mutation the algorithm is already exploitative and more elite tips it
#       into stagnation; with an unmutated alpha caste (pure elitism) the gain
#       per evaluation is largest.
#     - The old prop_mediated (mediated / total) is meaningless when the two
#       paths have opposite signs (alpha, population_size); it is replaced by
#       budget_share = |mediated| / (|direct| + |mediated|). Report both
#       components anyway.
#     E/E readings are interpretations consistent with the signs, not a direct
#     measurement of exploration/exploitation.

# ---- 4. Mediation per version and parameter (bootstrap) ----------------------
#
# Within each version: mediator (evaluation budget) model log_evals ~ params^2
# and outcome model log_fit ~ params^2 + log_evals. For a two-level parameter,
# effects are average marginal effects of switching it from its low to its high
# level, averaged over the observed values of the other parameters. All effects
# are differences in log10(diff_fitness), i.e. log10 of the high/low fitness
# ratio.
#   same_budget:  DIRECT effect, at the same number of evaluations (search
#                 efficiency).
#   via_budget:   MEDIATED (indirect) effect, through the mediator, the
#                 evaluation budget = (change in log10 evaluations) x b, where
#                 b = slope of fitness on the mediator (negative). A positive
#                 value means the parameter shortens runs (fewer evaluations)
#                 and that costs fitness.
#   budget_share: share mediated = |via_budget| / (|same_budget| + |via_budget|),
#                 in [0, 1]; replaces mediated/total, which is meaningless when
#                 the two paths have opposite signs.
# With a single mediator slope b, total = same_budget + via_budget exactly
# (OLS, same regressors).

marginal_effect <- function(model, data, param) {
  lv <- levels(data[[param]])
  hi <- lo <- data
  hi[[param]] <- factor(lv[2], levels = lv)
  lo[[param]] <- factor(lv[1], levels = lv)
  mean(predict(model, hi) - predict(model, lo))
}

mediation_effects <- function(data) {
  f_m <- as.formula(paste("log_evals ~", param_terms))
  f_y <- as.formula(paste("log_fit ~", param_terms, "+ log_evals"))
  f_t <- as.formula(paste("log_fit ~", param_terms))
  mm <- lm(f_m, data); my <- lm(f_y, data); mt <- lm(f_t, data)
  b <- coef(my)["log_evals"]
  do.call(rbind, lapply(params, function(p) {
    total       <- marginal_effect(mt, data, p)
    same_budget <- marginal_effect(my, data, p)
    via_budget  <- b * marginal_effect(mm, data, p)
    data.frame(param = p, total = total, same_budget = same_budget,
               via_budget = unname(via_budget))
  }))
}

mediation_by_group <- function(g) {
  d <- fitness_data %>% filter(group == g) %>% droplevels()
  point <- mediation_effects(d)
  boots <- replicate(N_BOOT,
                     as.matrix(mediation_effects(d[sample(nrow(d), replace = TRUE), ])[, -1]),
                     simplify = "array")
  ci <- function(k, q) apply(boots[, k, ], 1, quantile, q)
  point %>%
    mutate(group = g,
           total_lo       = ci("total", 0.025),       total_hi       = ci("total", 0.975),
           same_budget_lo = ci("same_budget", 0.025), same_budget_hi = ci("same_budget", 0.975),
           via_budget_lo  = ci("via_budget", 0.025),  via_budget_hi  = ci("via_budget", 0.975),
           budget_share   = abs(via_budget) / (abs(same_budget) + abs(via_budget))) %>%
    relocate(group)
}

mediation_table <- bind_rows(lapply(levels(fitness_data$group), mediation_by_group))

# cat("\nEffect on log10(diff_fitness) of switching each parameter low -> high",
#     "(negative = better fitness): same_budget = direct, via_budget = mediated",
#     "by the evaluation budget, 95% bootstrap CI:\n")
# print(mediation_table %>%
#         transmute(group, param,
#                   total       = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", total, total_lo, total_hi),
#                   same_budget = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", same_budget, same_budget_lo, same_budget_hi),
#                   via_budget  = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", via_budget, via_budget_lo, via_budget_hi),
#                   budget_share = round(budget_share, 2)),
#       row.names = FALSE)

# ---- 5. Version effects (consecutive pairs, as in the paper) ------------------
#
# Same decomposition, with the version change as the "treatment" and the four
# parameters as covariates. microopt vs baseline is a negative control:
# an implementation-only change should have ~0 total and direct (same_budget)
# effect.

version_pairs <- list(c("baseline", "gamma_upgrade"),
                      c("baseline", "microopt"),
                      c("microopt", "low_mutation"),
                      c("low_mutation", "no_mutation_alpha"))

version_effect <- function(data) {
  mm <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_evals ~ group *", param_terms)), data)
  my <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ group *", param_terms, "+ log_evals")), data)
  mt <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ group *", param_terms)), data)
  b <- coef(my)["log_evals"]
  c(total       = marginal_effect(mt, data, "group"),
    same_budget = marginal_effect(my, data, "group"),
    via_budget  = unname(b * marginal_effect(mm, data, "group")))
}

version_table <- do.call(rbind, lapply(version_pairs, function(pair) {
  d <- fitness_data %>% filter(group %in% pair) %>%
    mutate(group = factor(as.character(group), levels = pair))
  point <- version_effect(d)
  boots <- replicate(N_BOOT, version_effect(d[sample(nrow(d), replace = TRUE), ]))
  q <- apply(boots, 1, quantile, c(0.025, 0.975))
  data.frame(change = paste(pair, collapse = " -> "),
             total       = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", point["total"],       q[1, "total"],       q[2, "total"]),
             same_budget = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", point["same_budget"], q[1, "same_budget"], q[2, "same_budget"]),
             via_budget  = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", point["via_budget"],  q[1, "via_budget"],  q[2, "via_budget"]),
             budget_share = round(abs(point["via_budget"]) /
                                  (abs(point["same_budget"]) + abs(point["via_budget"])), 2),
             row.names = NULL)
}))

#cat("\nEffect of each version change on log10(diff_fitness), 95% bootstrap CI:\n")
#print(version_table, row.names = FALSE)

# ---- 5b. Version effects with a version-specific convergence slope ------------
#
# Section 5 uses one slope b of fitness on evaluations for both versions in a
# pair, so via_budget values every extra evaluation at the same rate in both,
# while F2 shows the slopes differ ~4x (microopt -> low_mutation) and ~2x
# (low_mutation -> no_mutation_alpha). Here the outcome model gets a
# group:log_evals interaction, so each version has its own slope, and the
# question becomes: are the new version's extra evaluations worth more?
#
# With M_old / M_new = predicted log10(evaluations) of each configuration under
# the old / new version (mediator model), and b_old / b_new the two slopes:
#   same_budget:    new - old version at the OLD version's budget M_old
#                   (better search at the same effort).
#   via_budget:     b_new x (M_new - M_old): what the new version gains from
#                   its change in run length, at its own rate. Split into
#     at_old_rate:  b_old x (M_new - M_old): what those extra evaluations would
#                   have bought the old version;
#     better_use:   (b_new - b_old) x (M_new - M_old): extra gain because the
#                   new version converts evaluations into fitness faster.
#   slope_old, slope_new, slope_diff: the two convergence rates (log-log).
# total is still the model without the mediator; same_budget + via_budget no
# longer has to equal it exactly (it does only for parallel slopes).

version_effect_slopes <- function(data) {
  pair <- levels(data$group)
  mm <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_evals ~ group *", param_terms)), data)
  my <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ group *", param_terms, "+ group * log_evals")), data)
  mt <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ group *", param_terms)), data)
  old <- new <- data
  old$group <- factor(pair[1], levels = pair)
  new$group <- factor(pair[2], levels = pair)
  m_old <- predict(mm, old)
  m_new <- predict(mm, new)
  slope <- function(d) {                   # model is linear in log_evals
    d1 <- d; d1$log_evals <- d$log_evals + 1
    mean(predict(my, d1) - predict(my, d))
  }
  new_at_old <- new
  old$log_evals        <- m_old
  new_at_old$log_evals <- m_old
  new$log_evals        <- m_new
  b_old <- slope(old); b_new <- slope(new)
  d_m   <- mean(m_new - m_old)
  c(total       = mean(predict(mt, new) - predict(mt, old)),
    same_budget = mean(predict(my, new_at_old) - predict(my, old)),
    via_budget  = b_new * d_m,
    at_old_rate = b_old * d_m,
    better_use  = (b_new - b_old) * d_m,
    slope_old   = b_old,
    slope_new   = b_new,
    slope_diff  = b_new - b_old)
}

fmt_ci <- function(x, lo, hi) sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", x, lo, hi)

version_slope_results <- lapply(version_pairs, function(pair) {
  d <- fitness_data %>% filter(group %in% pair) %>%
    mutate(group = factor(as.character(group), levels = pair))
  point <- version_effect_slopes(d)
  boots <- replicate(N_BOOT, version_effect_slopes(d[sample(nrow(d), replace = TRUE), ]))
  q <- apply(boots, 1, quantile, c(0.025, 0.975))
  list(change = paste(pair, collapse = " -> "), point = point, q = q)
})

version_slope_table <- do.call(rbind, lapply(version_slope_results, function(r) {
  cols <- c("total", "same_budget", "via_budget", "at_old_rate", "better_use")
  as.data.frame(c(list(change = r$change),
                  setNames(lapply(cols, function(k) fmt_ci(r$point[k], r$q[1, k], r$q[2, k])), cols)))
}))

version_slope_rates <- do.call(rbind, lapply(version_slope_results, function(r) {
  cols <- c("slope_old", "slope_new", "slope_diff")
  as.data.frame(c(list(change = r$change),
                  setNames(lapply(cols, function(k) fmt_ci(r$point[k], r$q[1, k], r$q[2, k])), cols)))
}))

version_label <- function(version) sprintf("{\\sf %s}", gsub("_", "\\\\_", version))

estimate_with_interval <- function(result, column) {
  kableExtra::linebreak(sprintf("%.3f\n{\\scriptsize [%.3f, %.3f]}",
                                result$point[column], result$q[1, column], result$q[2, column]),
                        align = "c")
}

# diff_fitness is a distance to the optimum (lower = better), so a log10 effect
# of e means the new version's diff_fitness is 10^e times the old one; the
# improvement factor is 10^(-e). The interval bounds swap when negated.
improvement_factor_with_interval <- function(result, column) {
  kableExtra::linebreak(sprintf("%.2f$\\times$\n{\\scriptsize [%.2f, %.2f]}",
                                10^(-result$point[column]),
                                10^(-result$q[2, column]), 10^(-result$q[1, column])),
                        align = "c")
}

version_slope_kable <- do.call(rbind, lapply(version_slope_results, function(result) {
  versions_in_pair <- strsplit(result$change, " -> ", fixed = TRUE)[[1]]
  data.frame(change      = paste(version_label(versions_in_pair), collapse = " $\\to$ "),
             total       = estimate_with_interval(result, "total"),
             same_budget = estimate_with_interval(result, "same_budget"),
             same_budget_improvement = improvement_factor_with_interval(result, "same_budget"),
             via_budget  = estimate_with_interval(result, "via_budget"),
             via_budget_improvement = improvement_factor_with_interval(result, "via_budget"))
}))

version_slope_kable %>%
  kableExtra::kbl(format = "latex", booktabs = TRUE, escape = FALSE, align = "lccccc",
                  row.names = FALSE, label = "evoapps27.version.slopes",
                  col.names = c("Version change", "Total", "Effect", "Fitness improvement",
                                "Effect", "Fitness improvement"),
                  caption = paste("Effect of each version change on $\\log_{10}$ fitness difference",
                                  "(negative = better fitness) with version-specific convergence",
                                  "slopes, split into the direct effect at the same number of",
                                  "evaluations and the effect via the number of evaluations.",
                                  "Fitness improvement is how many times closer to the optimum",
                                  "the new version gets through each path: with the same number",
                                  "of evaluations, and through the change in the number of",
                                  "evaluations it uses (ratio of geometric means; above 1 = better).",
                                  "95\\% bootstrap confidence intervals in brackets.")) %>%
  kableExtra::add_header_above(c(" " = 2, "Same evaluations" = 2, "Via evaluations" = 2),
                               bold = TRUE) %>%
  kableExtra::row_spec(0, bold = TRUE) %>%
  kableExtra::kable_styling(latex_options = c("scale_down", "hold_position"), font_size = 8)
#cat("\nConvergence slopes (log10 fitness per log10 evaluation) in each pair, 95% bootstrap CI:\n")
#print(version_slope_rates, row.names = FALSE)
@

How these two different effects are distributed for the 5 experiment levels is shown in Table \ref{tab:evoapps27.table}. We examine how the effect is distributed via same-budget (same number of evaluations), and extra effect achieved via evaluations; every level is compared with the one from which it is an improvement. The first row shows how the so-called gamma "upgrade" is essentially neutral; it shows a very small improvement, which is in fact compatible with no improvement at all. Changing the $\gamma$ layer does not change the efficiency of the algorithm

Surprisingly, making only improvements to the implementation improves slightly the fitness, around 35\% due entirely to same-budget effects. We do not have a clear explanation for this, other than a change in implementation might have fixed some unknown bug that made some operator underperform. At any rate, it is a very small and scarcely noticeable improvement.

Changing to a lower mutation rate, however (third row) improves same-budget results, with fitness reached 10 times better (that is, 1/10th of what was previously reached), with an additional improvement obtained via enabling longer runs, with more evaluations; fitness difference is almost halved that way.

The definitive improvement comes from suppressing mutation in the $\alpha$ layer: fitness is two orders of magnitude better via same-budget, with an additional (roughly) order of magnitude due to the fact that every run lasts longer, with more evaluations sampling additional areas of the search space.

<<evoapps27.plot, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warnings=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tb", fig.cap="Logarithmic plot of evaluations vs. fitness for the 5 different experiment levels, showing the fitted slope.">>=
# ---- 6. Plot ------------------------------------------------------------------

ggplot(fitness_data, aes(x = log_evals, y = log_fit, color = group)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.15, size = 0.8) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE, formula = y ~ x) +
  labs(x = "log10(evaluations)", y = "log10(diff_fitness)", color = "Version") +
  theme_minimal() + theme(legend.position = "bottom")
@

This effect can be observed in Figure \ref{fig:evoapps27.plot}. When experimental points run more to the right, it is a signal of that additional number of evaluations obtained when the algorithm is not stuck in a local minima and new, better individuals are being found. The {\em slope} is also what shows how much fitness is being improved by every additional evaluation. The steeper slope is shown by the {\sf no\_alpha\_mutation} experiment (lower cloud, in pink). However, we see that the slope cuts across two different clusters of experiments; as a matter of fact, the vertical and horizontal extension might indicate some internal dynamics that might be interesting to investigate.

<<evoapps27.alpha, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warnings=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tb", fig.cap="Logarithmic plot of evaluations vs. fitness for the 5 different experiment levels, showing the fitted slope.">>=
# ---- 7. no_mutation_alpha: which parameter splits the fitness/evaluations cloud?
#
# In the plot above the no_mutation_alpha cloud is visibly split in two
# parallel bands. A parameter that creates the split shifts fitness *at the
# same number of evaluations*, i.e. it acts through the direct (same_budget)
# path, not through the mediator (evaluation budget).
# For each parameter:
#   - offset: difference in mean residual of log_fit ~ log_evals between its
#     levels (vertical distance between the two bands, in log10 units);
#   - r2_gain: extra R^2 of log_fit ~ log_evals + param over log_fit ~ log_evals;
#   - agreement: how well its levels match a 2-cluster k-means split of those
#     residuals (0.5 = unrelated, 1 = the parameter *is* the split).

nma_data <- fitness_data %>% filter(group == "no_mutation_alpha") %>% droplevels()

m_nma_evals   <- lm(log_fit ~ log_evals, data = nma_data)
nma_data$res  <- resid(m_nma_evals)
r2_evals     <- summary(m_nma_evals)$r.squared
set.seed(1)
nma_clusters  <- kmeans(nma_data$res, centers = quantile(nma_data$res, c(0.25, 0.75)))$cluster

split_table <- do.call(rbind, lapply(params, function(p) {
  lv  <- levels(nma_data[[p]])
  tab <- table(nma_data[[p]], nma_clusters)
  data.frame(param     = p,
             offset    = mean(nma_data$res[nma_data[[p]] == lv[2]]) -
                         mean(nma_data$res[nma_data[[p]] == lv[1]]),
             r2_gain   = summary(lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~ log_evals +", p)),
                                    nma_data))$r.squared - r2_evals,
             agreement = max(sum(diag(tab)), sum(tab) - sum(diag(tab))) / sum(tab))
})) %>% arrange(desc(agreement))

# cat("\nno_mutation_alpha: separation of the fitness bands by parameter",
#     "(offset = high - low level, log10 units):\n")
# print(split_table, digits = 3, row.names = FALSE)
split_param <- split_table$param[1]
# cat("Parameter that best explains the split:", split_param, "\n")

# Mediation by the evaluation budget for no_mutation_alpha (from section 4):
# the split parameter should show a large *direct* (same_budget) effect.
# cat("\nno_mutation_alpha: total / direct (same_budget) / mediated (via_budget)",
#     "effect of each parameter (from section 4):\n")
# print(mediation_table %>%
#         filter(group == "no_mutation_alpha") %>%
#         transmute(param,
#                   total       = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", total, total_lo, total_hi),
#                   same_budget = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", same_budget, same_budget_lo, same_budget_hi),
#                   via_budget  = sprintf("%.3f [%.3f, %.3f]", via_budget, via_budget_lo, via_budget_hi),
#                   budget_share = round(budget_share, 2)),
#       row.names = FALSE)

# Convergence line (log_fit ~ log_evals) for every level of every parameter.
# Pooling the two bands flattens the slope, so within-level slopes for the
# split parameter are the more meaningful convergence rates.
nma_long <- bind_rows(lapply(params, function(p) {
  nma_data %>% transmute(param = p, level = as.character(.data[[p]]), log_evals, log_fit)
}))

nma_level_slopes <- nma_long %>%
  group_by(param, level) %>%
  group_modify(function(d, key) {
    m  <- lm(log_fit ~ log_evals, d)
    ci <- confint(m)["log_evals", ]
    data.frame(n = nrow(d), intercept = coef(m)[[1]], slope = coef(m)[[2]],
               lower = ci[[1]], upper = ci[[2]])
  }) %>%
  ungroup()
# cat("\nno_mutation_alpha: log10(diff_fitness) ~ log10(evaluations) per parameter level:\n")
# print(as.data.frame(nma_level_slopes), digits = 3, row.names = FALSE)

# Mediation for the other parameters *within* each level of the split
# parameter, so their effects are not confounded with the band they fall in.
other_params <- setdiff(params, split_param)
# cat("\nno_mutation_alpha: effects of the remaining parameters within each level of", split_param, "\n")
within_split <- bind_rows(lapply(levels(nma_data[[split_param]]), function(l) {
  d  <- nma_data %>% filter(.data[[split_param]] == l)
  pt <- paste0("(", paste(other_params, collapse = " + "), ")^2")
  mm <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_evals ~", pt)), d)
  mt <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~",   pt)), d)
  my <- lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~",   pt, "+ log_evals")), d)
  b  <- coef(my)[["log_evals"]]
  do.call(rbind, lapply(other_params, function(p) {
    data.frame(level = paste(split_param, "=", l), param = p,
               total       = marginal_effect(mt, d, p),
               same_budget = marginal_effect(my, d, p),
               via_budget  = b * marginal_effect(mm, d, p))
  }))
}))
# print(within_split, digits = 3, row.names = FALSE)

ggplot(nma_long, aes(log_evals, log_fit, colour = level)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.15, size = 0.7) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x, se = FALSE, linewidth = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~ param, nrow = 1) +
  labs(x = "log10(evaluations)", y = "log10(diff_fitness)", colour = "Level",
       title = "no_mutation_alpha: fitness vs evaluations by parameter level") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
@

The first analysis looks for an explanation of the "split": Figure \ref{fig:evoapps27.alpha} plots with different colors high-low values of the different parameters, along with the respective log-log fitness-evaluations slope for them. The {\sf alpha} value is the first one, and clearly explains the split in two clusters. The slopes are very similar; however, the higher value where the $\alpha$ layer gets 25\% of the population clearly needs less evaluations to reach the same fitness. All other parameters being equal, same-budget fitness reached favors this configuration.

The same happens for the other parameters, except for {\sf steps}, that shows no difference. The slopes are the same, but the distribution of evaluations/fitness reached depends on the value; the slopes are not significant, because they cut across clusters thus not being really relevant for the different mechanism used to reach better fitness values.

We can attribute the relative failure of moving the $\gamma$ caste to this observed effect of the number of steps in the overall result, which seems indifferent to the value. This is probably due to the fact that the actual number of hillclimbing steps is lower than the lowest value (16). This, however, needs to be investigated further.

<<evoapps27.alpha.fwl, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, fig.height=4, fig.pos="h!tb", fig.cap="FWL view of the $\\alpha$ caste size in the {\\sf no\\_mutation\\_alpha} version: log-log plot of residuals + averages, with the FWL decomposition shown on the chart.">>=
# Why do alpha = 10% and alpha = 25% differ if their convergence slopes are
# the same? Equal slopes mean both convert extra evaluations into fitness at
# the same rate; the difference must then be in the intercept (fitness at the
# same budget) and in where along the line the runs stop (the budget).
# FWL: remove max_gens, population_size and steps from both axes, then fit
#   fitness_net ~ alpha + evaluations_net
# with a single slope. The alpha coefficient is the same-budget effect; the
# alpha shift in evaluations_net times the slope is the budget-mediated
# effect; both add up exactly to the difference between the alpha centroids.
other_parameter_terms <- paste0("(", paste(setdiff(params, "alpha"), collapse = " + "), ")^2")

alpha_net_runs <- nma_data %>%
  mutate(fitness_net     = resid(lm(as.formula(paste("log_fit ~",   other_parameter_terms)), nma_data)) +
                           mean(log_fit),
         evaluations_net = resid(lm(as.formula(paste("log_evals ~", other_parameter_terms)), nma_data)) +
                           mean(log_evals))

common_slope_model <- lm(fitness_net ~ alpha + evaluations_net, data = alpha_net_runs)
common_slope       <- coef(common_slope_model)[["evaluations_net"]]
same_budget_effect <- coef(common_slope_model)[["alpha25"]]

alpha_centroids <- alpha_net_runs %>%
  group_by(alpha) %>%
  summarise(evaluations_net = mean(evaluations_net),
            fitness_net     = mean(fitness_net), .groups = "drop")

low_alpha_centroid  <- alpha_centroids %>% filter(alpha == "10")
high_alpha_centroid <- alpha_centroids %>% filter(alpha == "25")
evaluations_shift   <- high_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net - low_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net
via_budget_effect   <- common_slope * evaluations_shift
total_effect        <- high_alpha_centroid$fitness_net - low_alpha_centroid$fitness_net

# Corner of the path: 10% line evaluated at the 25% budget
path_corner <- data.frame(evaluations_net = high_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net,
                          fitness_net     = low_alpha_centroid$fitness_net + via_budget_effect)

alpha_lines <- alpha_centroids %>%
  mutate(intercept = fitness_net - common_slope * evaluations_net)

effect_path <- data.frame(
  path  = c("via budget", "same budget", "total"),
  x     = c(low_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net, path_corner$evaluations_net,
            low_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net),
  y     = c(low_alpha_centroid$fitness_net, path_corner$fitness_net,
            low_alpha_centroid$fitness_net),
  xend  = c(path_corner$evaluations_net, high_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net,
            high_alpha_centroid$evaluations_net),
  yend  = c(path_corner$fitness_net, high_alpha_centroid$fitness_net,
            high_alpha_centroid$fitness_net),
  label = c(sprintf("via budget: %+.2f\n(%.0f%% fewer evaluations)",
                    via_budget_effect, 100 * (1 - 10^evaluations_shift)),
            sprintf("same budget: %+.2f\n(%.0fx better)",
                    same_budget_effect, 10^-same_budget_effect),
            sprintf("total: %+.2f\n(%.0fx better)",
                    total_effect, 10^-total_effect)))

ggplot(alpha_net_runs, aes(evaluations_net, fitness_net, colour = alpha)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.15, size = 0.7) +
  geom_abline(data = alpha_lines,
              aes(intercept = intercept, slope = common_slope, colour = alpha),
              linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_segment(data = effect_path,
               aes(x = x, y = y, xend = xend, yend = yend, linetype = path),
               colour = "grey15", linewidth = 0.6,
               arrow = arrow(length = unit(0.15, "cm")), inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  geom_label(data = effect_path,
             aes(x = (x + xend) / 2, y = (y + yend) / 2, label = label),
             size = 2.5, label.size = 0, fill = "white", alpha = 0.85,
             inherit.aes = FALSE) +
  geom_point(data = alpha_centroids, size = 3, shape = 21, fill = "white", stroke = 1.2) +
  scale_linetype_manual(values = c("via budget" = "solid", "same budget" = "solid",
                                   "total" = "dotted"), guide = "none") +
  guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(alpha = 1, size = 2))) +
  labs(x = "log10(evaluations) net of max_gens, population_size, steps",
       y = "log10(diff_fitness) net of other parameters",
       colour = "alpha caste (%)") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
@

We can again use the FWL theorem on this experiment and the difference between the two parameter levels, shown in Figure \ref{fig:evoapps27.alpha.fwl}, which plots the residuals of the two models for equal values of the other 3 parameters; in this case we are studying the {\sf alpha} percentage but again the mediator variable is the number of evaluations. The chart shows what explains the difference between the two values: when {\sf alpha} = 10\%, it needs more evaluations to achieve the same fitness level; its {\em path} to reach lower fitness levels is only by making more evaluations. For the same number of evaluations, {\sf alpha} = 25\% is 22 times better than the other value, which can keep the algorithm running for longer without stagnation, but is still unable to find better solutions or reach the part of the search space where they are placed. This can be explained, in turn, by fact that the $\gamma$ caste changes in the opposite direction of $\alpha$: when this takes 10\% of the population, $\gamma$ would have 60\%. The hillclimbing performed by this layer will still make evolution advance to lower fitness values, but at the cost of several evaluations.


\section{Discussion and conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}

% Future work: is the gamma caste useless? In the current design a single
% parameter sets every caste size (delta = epsilon = 5%, beta = 2 x alpha,
% gamma the rest), so going from alpha = 10% to alpha = 25% moves gamma from
% 60% to 15% of the population. The better same-budget fitness and the cheaper
% generations of alpha = 25% cannot then be attributed to a smaller gamma caste
% or to larger alpha and beta castes separately. Besides, the number of
% hill-climbing steps (16 vs. 64) has no measurable effect on fitness or
% evaluations, which suggests gamma's local search contributes little. Two
% experiments would settle this:
% (1) Decoupling the caste sizes: keep alpha fixed and move population between
%     beta and gamma (e.g. gamma = 15%, 35%, 60%), so the effect of gamma's size
%     can be measured with the FWL decomposition used here, at the same alpha.
% (2) Removing gamma's local search: run with steps = 0, or with no gamma caste
%     at all (its share given to beta or to the explorative delta/epsilon
%     castes). If same-budget fitness does not worsen, gamma is effectively
%     useless in this setup; if it does, gamma keeps some diversity that the
%     smallest size tested (15%) still provides.
% Both should be run for no_mutation_alpha, where the alpha effect is ~9x
% larger than in the other versions, and for at least one version with
% mutation in the alpha caste, to check whether the gain comes from removing
% gamma or from feeding a crossover-only alpha caste with a larger beta.

